{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T234737Z-988034f5", "ts": "2026-10-27T23:52:01Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "fix/1092-chunk-state-table"}, "body": {"base_commit": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "base_tree": "d28058350bc526e7fcfae56e34065884436ebebd", "head_tree": "8047cd1770e721f8b7f337b247c65fd92f88f954", "head_commit": "369e6f797f29abc7074653ec7bd362a5a0016963", "dispatch_commit": "369e6f797f29abc7074653ec7bd362a5a0016963", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_uncertified_chunks_close.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_uncertified_chunks_close.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A span credits a chunk by Deliverables path from a commit that is not that chunk's work", "recommendation": "Class. The cause: _Spans.carried hands _deliverable_chunks the files of every commit in the fact's interval, and the path half credits each path's sole owner no matter whose work the commit was. That breaks the plan's own claim that 'an unbuilt chunk has no commit, so a span can never certify it' (#1072's invariant) in two cases the case table has no row for. (a) Commits a base merge brought into the branch. A chunk/final review whose base is the frontier, or a verify-resolutions whose base is the prior head, taken after `git merge develop` has develop's commits in base_commit..head_commit. The interval diff includes their files, so the edge is valid, and a develop commit touching a path only chunk N lists marks N done, unbuilt. (b) A commit whose scoped subject names chunk 02 but which touches a path only chunk 03 lists credits 03 as well, against the commit's own attribution. In both cases the chunk reads done and is never reviewed again, and archive-plan stamps it built. The fix is a construction in the one owner: count only commits that are the branch's own work (for example, log the interval with --first-parent so merged-in commits drop out, failing closed), and apply the path half only to commits whose subject carries no chunk of this plan. Add case rows for both and a negative control for each. The proposal shares _deliverable_chunks over a tree diff, so check it against the same two shapes.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "A dirty-tree review dispatched at the fork point reads open after a --no-ff merge deletes the branch, a merge shape no row or consumer note covers", "recommendation": "Instance. Take a chunk whose review ran over uncommitted work before the branch's first commit. Its base_commit = dispatch_commit = the fork point. The tree is then committed verbatim and merged --no-ff, and the branch is deleted. The anchor is on main's first-parent history, so _Reachability refuses it, and the chunk flips from done to open: both the named answer and the span refuse it. That is #1079's symptom in a shape the fix does not reach. M8 tests only the unmerged fork-point case, and the fixtures deliberately avoid this one (_plan_commit puts a commit before every dirty review). The change record's consumer note tells users that only squash and fast-forward merges leave chunks open and to 'merge with a merge commit', which is false here. Either accept the anchor when the span's verbatim chain finds a commit off the base's first-parent history whose tree is head_tree (that commit proves the merge brought the reviewed tree in), or pin the shape as fail-closed with its own M row and widen the consumer note to say so.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Re-running `plan retract --fact` on an already-retracted span certification is refused as not-named instead of answering already", "recommendation": "Instance. retraction_target runs the not-named check before the already-retracted check. Once a span pair is retracted the chunk is open, so by_span is false, and an explicit --fact re-run exits 1 saying the fact 'does not certify it by its interval'. For a named pair the same re-run answers already and exits 0. A recorded chunk-retracted fact for (fact_id, chunk) already proves the fact certified the chunk. Check that pair before the not-named refusal and add the re-run assertion to X3.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'can this fact certify' predicate is written twice in chunk_states, once for naming and once for spanning", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The naming loop and the span loop each test the same preconditions in their own words: an unresolved blocker (verdicts), a plan with no branch:, an anchor that is not an object id, and reachable(anchor, base_commit). The naming loop turns each one into a reason, and the span loop folds them into one silent `continue` chain. Both are the authority rule for done, and nothing keeps them in step. Whoever adds a precondition next (a new refusal arm, say) will add it where a test fails, which is the naming loop. Spans would then keep certifying chunks under a condition that naming refuses. Pull out one `_cannot_certify(fact, body) -> str | None` that both loops call. The naming loop notes the reason it returns, and the span loop skips on it. That gives the rule one owner, and the reason becomes available to a span later at no extra cost, so prior R-12 is not re-raised here.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "_Reachability.holder is a side channel that is valid only after __call__ returned None, plus a pass-through _on_first_parent wrapper", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `spans.carried(body, reachable.holder(anchor))` works only because the line before it called `reachable(anchor, ...)` and got None. `_holder` is filled as a side effect of `_first_held` and of the base path. If the call order changes, holder() returns a stale or missing ref, and a dirty-tree span then quietly carries nothing. Having __call__ (or a sibling method) return the holding ref alongside the refusal would make that dependency visible in the code. `_Reachability._on_first_parent` only forwards to the module-level `on_first_parent`, and no test spies on it (grep tests/). Call the function directly. No fix is required: the order is local to one function and correct today.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "planning.md's Chunk state sentence leaves out that only a fully-reviewed edge may span", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Prose only. The code credits a span only to a fact that `coverage_algebra.review_edges` accepts: both trees recorded, and every judgeable changed file inside files_reviewed. The planning.md sentence says a chunk is done when a clear, reachable review 'reviewed a commit that builds it', with no edge condition. A builder whose under-reviewed fact leaves a chunk open will find nothing in the rule that explains it. The data-model.md extension and the plan_status docstring both state the condition. If it is fixed, the fix is the relational clause ('a review whose interval the coverage gate accepts'), not a narration. Otherwise accept.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/planning.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Ruling D1 is extended to certify unnamed chunks, and the only authority cited is a coordinator decision on this branch", "recommendation": "data-model.md's Direction ruling D1 (owner, 2026-10-24) says a chunk is done when a review fact NAMES it. This branch appends 'Extended 2026-10-27 (... the coordinator's design decision for #1082, owner request for 3.8.0): a review fact also completes a chunk it does not name ...'. That changes normative content. norms.md 'What counts as a recorded decision' requires an owner-visible, vetoable decision: a `[DECISION: ... | ... | user can veto/override]` block, or the owner making the call directly. It adds that 'a builder-authored rationale sentence, alone, is not a recorded decision.' The plan has only prose: 'Design decision (coordinator, for #1082's open fork): option 1'. Its own mid-build decisions say 'the owner's confirmation of option 1 lives with the coordinator, not here'. The owner asked for #1079 and #1082 to land in 3.8.0, and nothing records the owner choosing option 1. So the amendment's only witness is the branch that amends it, which is the core rule 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority: when an agent amends a binding norm mid-build, land the owner's confirmation somewhere the amendment isn't'. Appending beneath the clause rather than rewriting it follows the authoring rule 'Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless is the amend-to-match-own-code tell'. That fixes the form, but the authority is still missing. Two related gaps belong to the same owner decision. First, the Goals paragraph still marks the definition of 'the chunk's built commit' as *Inferred* as the whole-branch walk, and a mid-build decision replaced it with the fact's own interval plus the sole-owner path rule. Second, Level is still Medium, and the case table its 'what would raise it' names has been delivered. Remedy (instance): get the owner's confirmation of option 1 and of the interval/sole-owner definition, and record it outside this amendment: a vetoable [DECISION] block in the plan's Goals that the owner confirms, or the owner's ruling on #1082. Then cite that location in D1's extension note, resolve the *Inferred* marker and restate Level.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "governed-by-gap read 0 because record_lint cannot parse the plan's bare-path governed_by; three Direction-bearing artifacts have no dispositions", "recommendation": "The plan's frontmatter `governed_by:` lists bare paths (`- .prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md`, and so on). `record_lint._parse_governed_by` matches only `- artifact: <name>` entries (`_ARTIFACT_ENTRY_RE`), so it yields no entries, and the manifest reports `governed-by-gap: 0` as if every norm had been disposed of. In fact architecture.md, api-contract.md and data-model.md each carry a `## Direction` section, and the plan records no disposition (conforms / ruling needed / exception / amendment proposed / inapplicable) for any of their norms. One of them, D1, is the norm this change extends (finding above). This is the core rule 'A check never seen to return non-zero proves nothing': the zero is no answer. It is a class, not one instance: 12 of the 13 live plans with `governed_by:` use the bare-path form (grep `^governed_by:` -A2 over .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-*.md). Remedy, instance: rewrite this plan's block in the template's `- artifact: \u2026 dispositions:` form, with a line per Direction norm. Remedy, class (a construction): make `_check_governed_by` report an entry it cannot parse, such as a bare path or any list item that is not `artifact:`, as `governed-by-gap`, instead of returning no entries.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "A failed first-parent rev-list is reported as a fact about history", "recommendation": "When git fails or lists nothing, `plan_status.on_first_parent` returns True, so it fails closed. `_Reachability.__call__` then prints 'its commit is the review's own base commit, which is on `<base>`'s first-parent history, so the base held it before the review ran'. When git actually failed (row F1), that tells an operator the merge was a fast-forward or fork point, so they change their merge strategy instead of investigating a git error. Instance: return a tri-state, or a cause string, from `on_first_parent`, and word the refusal 'could not be placed (git failed: \u2026)' on the failure arm. This is distinct from the accepted R-12, which is about span refusals leaving no reason.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-9